---
import ToolShell from "../../components/ToolShell.astro";
---

<ToolShell
    slug="article-slug-generator"
    title="Article Slug Generator - SEO URL Slug Maker, Free & Online"
    metaDescription="Turn any article title into a clean, SEO-friendly URL slug. Strips stop words like a, the, of and in, handles accents and punctuation, and runs entirely in your browser."
    pageHeadingText="Article Slug Generator"
>
    <div slot="tool" class="flex flex-col gap-4">
        <div>
            <label class="tool-label" for="sl-in">Article title</label>
            <textarea
                id="sl-in"
                class="tool-input"
                style="min-height:5.5rem"
                spellcheck="false"
                placeholder="How to Install MongoDB on a VPS in 2026&#10;&#10;Paste one title per line to generate slugs in bulk."
            ></textarea>
        </div>

        <div class="tool-grid2">
            <div>
                <label class="tool-label" for="sl-sep">Separator</label>
                <select id="sl-sep" class="tool-select">
                    <option value="-">Hyphen - recommended</option>
                    <option value="_">Underscore</option>
                </select>
            </div>
            <div>
                <label class="tool-label" for="sl-max">Word limit (0 = no limit)</label>
                <input id="sl-max" class="tool-input" type="number" min="0" max="30" step="1" value="0" />
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
            <label class="tool-row" style="gap:.55rem;cursor:pointer">
                <input id="sl-stop" type="checkbox" checked />
                <span class="tool-note">Remove stop words - <code>a</code>, <code>the</code>, <code>of</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>with</code> and friends</span>
            </label>
            <label class="tool-row" style="gap:.55rem;cursor:pointer">
                <input id="sl-question" type="checkbox" />
                <span class="tool-note">Also remove question words - <code>how</code>, <code>what</code>, <code>why</code>, <code>when</code>. Off by default: they carry search intent.</span>
            </label>
        </div>

        <div>
            <label class="tool-label" for="sl-extra">Also remove these words</label>
            <input id="sl-extra" class="tool-input" placeholder="comma separated, e.g. best, ultimate, guide, 2026" />
        </div>

        <div>
            <label class="tool-label" for="sl-out">Slug</label>
            <textarea id="sl-out" class="tool-input" readonly style="min-height:4.5rem;font-size:.95rem"></textarea>
        </div>

        <div class="tool-row">
            <button id="sl-copy" class="tool-btn"><i class="fa-regular fa-copy"></i> Copy slug</button>
            <button id="sl-clear" class="tool-btn-ghost">Clear</button>
            <span id="sl-stats" class="tool-note"></span>
        </div>

        <p id="sl-before" class="tool-note" hidden></p>
        <div id="sl-removed" class="tool-row" style="display:none;gap:.35rem"></div>
        <p id="sl-warn" class="tool-error" hidden></p>
    </div>

    <h2>Turn any headline into a clean URL slug</h2>
    <p>
        A slug is the human-readable tail of a URL - the <code>how-to-install-mongodb-vps</code> in
        <code>example.com/articles/how-to-install-mongodb-vps</code>. This <strong>article slug generator</strong>
        takes a title, lowercases it, strips punctuation and accents, joins the words with hyphens and drops the
        filler words that add length without adding meaning. Paste one title or a hundred; everything happens in
        your browser and nothing is uploaded.
    </p>

    <h2>What are stop words, and do they hurt SEO?</h2>
    <p>
        Stop words are the high-frequency connective words a search engine can safely ignore: <em>a, an, the, of,
        in, on, for, with, is, are, to</em>. Leaving them in a URL will not get you penalised - Google's own
        guidance treats the URL as a minor ranking signal, and its crawlers parse
        <code>the-best-way-to-learn-html</code> perfectly well.
    </p>
    <p>
        The reason to strip them anyway is everything <em>around</em> the ranking:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Keyword prominence</strong> - a short slug puts your actual topic at the front, where both readers and search engines weigh it most.</li>
        <li><strong>Readability in results</strong> - Google truncates long URLs in the SERP, and a truncated URL is a weaker click signal.</li>
        <li><strong>Shareability</strong> - shorter links survive being pasted into chat, slides and print without wrapping or breaking.</li>
        <li><strong>Consistency</strong> - a rule applied by a tool beats a rule applied by hand at 11pm.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        So: worth doing, not worth agonising over. If dropping a word makes the slug read strangely, keep it.
    </p>

    <h3>The words this tool keeps on purpose</h3>
    <p>
        Most slug generators strip <code>how</code>, <code>what</code>, <code>why</code> and <code>when</code>
        along with everything else. That is usually a mistake. Those words <em>are</em> the search query for
        informational content - people type "how to install mongodb", not "install mongodb" - and a slug that
        mirrors the query is easier to scan in a list of results. This tool leaves them alone unless you tick the
        second box.
    </p>
    <p>
        Negations are protected for a blunter reason: <code>not</code>, <code>no</code> and <code>without</code>
        reverse the meaning of a title. "Why You Should Not Use jQuery" must never become
        <code>why-you-should-use-jquery</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>What the generator does to your title</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Normalises accented characters - <code>Café Décor</code> becomes <code>cafe-decor</code>, not <code>caf-dcor</code>.</li>
        <li>Expands <code>&amp;</code> into <code>and</code> - which then drops out as a stop word unless you untick the box.</li>
        <li>Deletes apostrophes without leaving a gap, so <code>developer's</code> becomes <code>developers</code> rather than <code>developer-s</code>.</li>
        <li>Replaces every remaining run of punctuation, symbols and whitespace with a single separator.</li>
        <li>Removes stop words - unless that would empty the slug, in which case it keeps them.</li>
        <li>Applies your word limit, lowercases everything, and trims stray separators from both ends.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Slug rules worth following</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Hyphens, never underscores.</strong> Google treats a hyphen as a word separator and an underscore as a joiner, so <code>web_design</code> can be read as one token.</li>
        <li><strong>Lowercase only.</strong> Some servers treat <code>/My-Post</code> and <code>/my-post</code> as different URLs, which splits your ranking signals between two pages.</li>
        <li><strong>Aim for three to six words.</strong> Long enough to describe the page, short enough to survive truncation.</li>
        <li><strong>Lead with the primary keyword.</strong> It is the first thing read on both sides of the search box.</li>
        <li><strong>Leave dates out</strong> unless the page really is tied to a year. <code>/best-laptops-2024</code> looks stale in 2026 even after you update the article.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not edit a published slug casually.</strong> Changing it creates a new URL and drops every link and ranking pointed at the old one. If you must, set up a 301 redirect.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>"The Ultimate Guide to CSS Grid for Beginners" → <code>ultimate-guide-css-grid-beginners</code></li>
        <li>"How to Install MongoDB on a VPS in 2026" → <code>how-install-mongodb-vps-2026</code></li>
        <li>"What's the Difference Between HTML Elements and Attributes?" → <code>whats-difference-html-elements-attributes</code></li>
        <li>"10 Things I Wish I Knew Before Learning React" → <code>10-things-wish-knew-learning-react</code></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Writing the article next?</h2>
    <p>
        Once the slug is settled, the <a href="/tools/seo-article-writer">SEO article writer</a> scores the draft
        itself - keyword density, headings, readability. For the rest of the URL, the
        <a href="/tools/url-encoder-decoder">URL encoder and decoder</a> handles query strings and percent-encoding.
    </p>

    <script is:inline>
    (function () {
        const $ = id => document.getElementById(id);

        // Connective words that add length without adding meaning. Question
        // words and negations are deliberately absent - see QUESTION_WORDS
        // below and the article above.
        const STOP_WORDS = new Set(`
            a about above after again against all am an and any are as at
            be because been before being below between both but by
            can cannot could did do does doing down during
            each few for from further
            had has have having he her here hers herself him himself his
            i if in into is it its itself
            let me more most my myself
            of off on once only or other ought our ours ourselves out over own
            same shall she should so some such
            than that the their theirs them themselves then there these they this those through to too
            under until up us
            very was we were will with would
            you your yours yourself yourselves
        `.trim().split(/\s+/));

        // Kept unless the second checkbox is ticked: these mirror how people
        // actually search for informational content.
        const QUESTION_WORDS = new Set(["how", "what", "why", "when", "where", "which", "who", "whom", "whose"]);

        function buildStopSet(includeQuestions, extraRaw) {
            const set = new Set(STOP_WORDS);
            if (includeQuestions) QUESTION_WORDS.forEach(word => set.add(word));
            String(extraRaw || "")
                .toLowerCase()
                .split(/[,\s]+/)
                .filter(Boolean)
                .forEach(word => set.add(word));
            return set;
        }

        function toWords(title) {
            return String(title)
                // Split accented characters into letter + accent, then drop the
                // accents, so "Café" survives as "cafe" rather than "caf".
                .normalize("NFKD")
                .replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g, "")
                .replace(/&/g, " and ")
                // Apostrophes close up: developer's -> developers.
                .replace(/['‘’ʼ`]/g, "")
                .toLowerCase()
                .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, " ")
                .trim()
                .split(/\s+/)
                .filter(Boolean);
        }

        function slugify(title, options) {
            const words = toWords(title);
            const removed = [];
            let kept = words;

            if (options.removeStopWords && words.length) {
                const filtered = words.filter(word => {
                    if (!options.stopSet.has(word)) return true;
                    removed.push(word);
                    return false;
                });
                // Stripping every word would leave no slug at all, so a title
                // made entirely of stop words keeps them.
                if (filtered.length) kept = filtered;
                else removed.length = 0;
            }

            if (options.maxWords > 0) kept = kept.slice(0, options.maxWords);

            return { slug: kept.join(options.separator), words: kept, removed: removed };
        }

        const LONG_SLUG = 60;

        function update() {
            const raw = $("sl-in").value;
            const separator = $("sl-sep").value;
            const maxWords = Math.max(0, Math.min(30, parseInt($("sl-max").value, 10) || 0));
            const options = {
                separator: separator,
                maxWords: maxWords,
                removeStopWords: $("sl-stop").checked,
                stopSet: buildStopSet($("sl-question").checked, $("sl-extra").value),
            };

            const titles = raw.split("\n").map(line => line.trim()).filter(Boolean);
            const results = titles.map(title => slugify(title, options));

            $("sl-out").value = results.map(result => result.slug).join("\n");

            const stats = $("sl-stats");
            const before = $("sl-before");
            const removedBox = $("sl-removed");
            const warn = $("sl-warn");

            if (!results.length) {
                stats.textContent = "";
                before.hidden = true;
                removedBox.style.display = "none";
                warn.hidden = true;
                return;
            }

            const allRemoved = [...new Set(results.flatMap(result => result.removed))];
            const longest = Math.max(...results.map(result => result.slug.length));
            const totalWords = results.reduce((sum, result) => sum + result.words.length, 0);

            stats.textContent = results.length > 1
                ? `${results.length} slugs - longest ${longest} characters - ${allRemoved.length} distinct words removed`
                : `${longest} characters - ${totalWords} words - ${results[0].removed.length} removed`;

            // The single-title case gets a before/after so the saving is obvious.
            const untouched = results.length === 1 && options.removeStopWords && results[0].removed.length
                ? slugify(titles[0], Object.assign({}, options, { removeStopWords: false }))
                : null;
            if (untouched) {
                before.hidden = false;
                before.textContent = `Without stop words removed: ${untouched.slug} (${untouched.slug.length} characters)`;
            } else {
                before.hidden = true;
            }

            if (allRemoved.length) {
                removedBox.style.display = "flex";
                removedBox.innerHTML =
                    '<span class="tool-note" style="margin-right:.25rem">Removed:</span>' +
                    allRemoved
                        .map(word => `<span class="sl-chip"></span>`)
                        .join("");
                // Removed words come from user input, so they are set as text.
                removedBox.querySelectorAll(".sl-chip").forEach((chip, index) => {
                    chip.textContent = allRemoved[index];
                });
            } else {
                removedBox.style.display = "none";
            }

            const emptyResult = results.some(result => !result.slug);
            if (emptyResult) {
                warn.hidden = false;
                warn.textContent = "Some titles produced an empty slug - they contain no Latin letters or digits.";
            } else if (longest > LONG_SLUG) {
                warn.hidden = false;
                warn.textContent = `Longest slug is ${longest} characters. Search results truncate long URLs - try a word limit of 5 or 6.`;
            } else {
                warn.hidden = true;
            }
        }

        ["sl-in", "sl-sep", "sl-max", "sl-stop", "sl-question", "sl-extra"].forEach(id => {
            $(id).addEventListener("input", update);
            $(id).addEventListener("change", update);
        });

        $("sl-copy").addEventListener("click", () => {
            const output = $("sl-out");
            if (!output.value) return;
            const notify = (message, type) => {
                if (window.showNotification) window.showNotification(message, type);
            };
            navigator.clipboard.writeText(output.value).then(
                () => notify("Slug copied to your clipboard.", "success"),
                () => {
                    // Denied permission or an insecure context - select the text
                    // so the copy is one keystroke away instead of a dead button.
                    output.focus();
                    output.select();
                    notify("Could not reach the clipboard - the slug is selected, press Ctrl+C.", "warning");
                },
            );
        });

        $("sl-clear").addEventListener("click", () => {
            $("sl-in").value = "";
            $("sl-extra").value = "";
            update();
            $("sl-in").focus();
        });

        $("sl-in").value = "The Ultimate Guide to CSS Grid for Beginners";
        update();
    })();
    </script>

    <style is:inline>
        .sl-chip {
            display: inline-block;
            padding: .1rem .5rem;
            border-radius: 9999px;
            font-size: .75rem;
            font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
            background: var(--tool-btn-ghost-bg);
            color: var(--tool-btn-ghost-text);
            border: 1px solid var(--tool-btn-ghost-border);
            text-decoration: line-through;
        }
        #sl-removed { flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; }
    </style>
</ToolShell>
